-- title:
--   A polynomial lies in the ideal $(X-x,\,Y-y)$ if and only if it vanishes at $(x,y)$
-- statement:
--   Let $P\in\mathbb{C}[X,Y]$ and $(x,y)\in\mathbb{C}^2$, and let $\mathfrak{m}=(X-x,\,Y-y)$ be the ideal of $\mathbb{C}[X,Y]$ generated by $X-x$ and $Y-y$.
--
--   Then
--
--   $$P\in\mathfrak{m}\iff P(x,y)=0.$$
--
--   This basic fact identifies $\mathfrak{m}$ as the ideal of polynomials vanishing at $(x,y)$, the maximal ideal used to define multiplicities of points in the treatment of the ordinary multiple points of Lemma 4 of the note.
-- source:
--   C. Perassi, Sharp symmetry bounds for real algebraic curves (note): lemma of the formalization, for Lemma 4, p. 3, https://github.com/carlok/curve-symmetry-lean/blob/d99bc17a1c397956c05d7417de50f9beed56580f/sharp_symmetry_bounds.pdf. Lean: https://github.com/carlok/curve-symmetry-lean/blob/d99bc17a1c397956c05d7417de50f9beed56580f/lean/OrdinaryMultiplePoints.lean (C. Perassi)

